{"id":"s5.1.7","slug":"double-integrals","title":"Double Integrals over Rectangles","content":"<p class=\"mb-4\">Over a rectangle with constant limits, a double integral of a separable integrand splits into a product of two ordinary integrals: $\\int_a^b \\int_c^d f(x)g(y)\\,dy\\,dx = \\left(\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx\\right)\\left(\\int_c^d g(y)\\,dy\\right)$.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">So $\\int_0^1 \\int_1^2 xy\\,dy\\,dx = \\left(\\int_0^1 x\\,dx\\right)\\left(\\int_1^2 y\\,dy\\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{3}{4}$.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">Do the two single integrals mentally and multiply. Never expand the inner integral symbolically first — that is where the time goes.</p>","summary":"Over a rectangle with constant limits, a double integral of a separable integrand splits into a product of two ordinary integrals: .","generation_guideline":"Generate a separable double integral over a rectangle with small integer limits.","questions":[{"id":1,"text":"$\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{2}^{4} x^2y^2\\,dy\\,dx = $ ______","answer":"168","kind":"exact","tags":["calculus"]},{"id":2,"text":"$\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{1}^{2} x^2y\\,dy\\,dx = $ ______","answer":"1/2","kind":"exact","tags":["calculus"]},{"id":3,"text":"$\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{2}^{4} x^2y^2\\,dy\\,dx = $ ______","answer":"56/9","kind":"exact","tags":["calculus"]},{"id":4,"text":"$\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} x^2y\\,dy\\,dx = $ ______","answer":"1/6","kind":"exact","tags":["calculus"]},{"id":5,"text":"$\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} xy\\,dy\\,dx = $ ______","answer":"9/4","kind":"exact","tags":["calculus"]},{"id":6,"text":"$\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{1}^{2} x^2y^2\\,dy\\,dx = $ ______","answer":"21","kind":"exact","tags":["calculus"]},{"id":7,"text":"$\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{2}^{3} x^2y^2\\,dy\\,dx = $ ______","answer":"57","kind":"exact","tags":["calculus"]},{"id":8,"text":"$\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} xy^2\\,dy\\,dx = $ ______","answer":"3/2","kind":"exact","tags":["calculus"]},{"id":9,"text":"$\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} xy\\,dy\\,d